    "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value",
    "problem": "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$",
